11
00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.150
<v Speaker 1>Allahu Majal Namin Alladina Amanu Amil Salihat.

12
00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:24.190
<v Speaker 1>Watawa Sabbil Haq.

13
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:25.630
<v Speaker 1>Watawa Sabr.

14
00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:27.790
<v Speaker 1>Amin Ya Rabbil Alamin.

15
00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:30.990
<v Speaker 1>Salam Alaikum warahmatullahi wabarakatu.

16
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:37.150
<v Speaker 1>There's a Hadith which is called Hadith Gibral.

17
00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:39.190
<v Speaker 1>It's a very famous Hadith.

18
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:44.270
<v Speaker 1>where Jibreel comes to the Prophet in the form of a man.

19
00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.430
<v Speaker 1>And he asks him four questions.

20
00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.350
<v Speaker 1>What is Islam?

21
00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.310
<v Speaker 1>What is Iman?

22
00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:50.190
<v Speaker 1>What is Ihsan?

23
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.310
<v Speaker 1>And then he asks him about the signs of the end of time.

24
00:02:55.710 --> 00:03:00.070
<v Speaker 1>So when, I mean, you can look it up and you know, you can get all the information about it.

25
00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:09.390
<v Speaker 2>I'm just going to talk about Islam, that when he was asked about Islam, the Prophet replied, Al Islamu an tashhada an la ilaha illallah wa anna Muhammad Rasool Allah.

26
00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:13.790
<v Speaker 2>He said, Islam is to bear witness that there is no God but Allah.

27
00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.790
<v Speaker 2>And Muhammad Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam says, Messenger.

28
00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.590
<v Speaker 2>Wa tuqima salah, to establish prayer.

29
00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.150
<v Speaker 2>Wa tuqti zakat, to give alms.

30
00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:27.390
<v Speaker 2>Wa tasuma Ramadan, to fast the 30 days in Ramadan.

31
00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.510
<v Speaker 2>Wa tu hajjal bayt in his ta'ata ilahi sabilah.

32
00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:35.390
<v Speaker 2>And to perform the pilgrimage to the House of God if you have the means.

33
00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:49.870
<v Speaker 2>Now what's interesting about this Hadith is that rather than giving a definition of Islam, which I would have expected, Rasulullah Alaihi Salaam, he described it as a set of practices.

34
00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:57.350
<v Speaker 2>In other words, what he's saying is that Islam is work, right?

35
00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:01.790
<v Speaker 2>But what we forget is that this is not how Islam started.

36
00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:06.350
<v Speaker 2>Islam as a human phenomenon, it

37
00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:12.830
<v Speaker 2>began with an incredible experience for the Prophet and then finally culminated in a set of practices.

38
00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:15.950
<v Speaker 2>For most of us, Islam begins as a practice.

39
00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:20.750
<v Speaker 2>It never begins for us as an experience, but that's not how it began for the Prophet.

40
00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:22.750
<v Speaker 2>How did it begin for him?

41
00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:30.350
<v Speaker 2>It began with a direct experience of the noumenal realm, right?

42
00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:33.310
<v Speaker 2>So you have the phenomenal realm, which is this world.

43
00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:38.270
<v Speaker 2>This world of appearances or what we call the seen world, Halim al-Shahada.

44
00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:43.550
<v Speaker 2>And then there is the noumenal realm, which is the unseen world, right?

45
00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:52.830
<v Speaker 2>Halim al-Gaib, which constitutes the metaphysical realities like God, angels, heaven, hell, all those things.

46
00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.630
<v Speaker 2>And the Prophet experienced both in his lifetime.

47
00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:01.470
<v Speaker 2>He had the phenomenal experience in the cave of Hira.

48
00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:07.230
<v Speaker 2>And then he also had the numinal experience with his ascension, right, the Maharaj.

49
00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:14.670
<v Speaker 2>So in today's session, what I wanted to do is I want us to connect with both those experiences.

50
00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:21.790
<v Speaker 2>Sort of step in his shoes and try to understand what it must have felt like.

51
00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:31.070
<v Speaker 2>Because his experiences, particularly these two experiences, they set a precedent for us in terms of what Islam is meant to feel like.

52
00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:41.790
<v Speaker 2>We all know what it is in terms of practice, but it's important for us to understand what the feeling or the experience of Islam is as it was for the Prophet.

53
00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:50.030
<v Speaker 2>So that once we connect with that, then we will talk about how we can use his experience for our own benefit.

54
00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:55.470
<v Speaker 2>So the year is 620 AD.

55
00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:00.510
<v Speaker 2>Our Prophet Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam, he's 40 years old.

56
00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:06.510
<v Speaker 2>Socially, the culture that he lives in is very troublesome for him.

57
00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:14.270
<v Speaker 2>He's bothered by the behavior of these Jahili Arabs, especially idol worship.

58
00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:21.790
<v Speaker 2>because he had a natural disinclination towards superstitions or any kind of animism for that matter.

59
00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:25.950
<v Speaker 2>So he's troubled, right?

60
00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:28.910
<v Speaker 2>And when you're troubled, what do you do?

61
00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:29.710
<v Speaker 2>You retreat.

62
00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:34.190
<v Speaker 2>So he wanted to get away from it all.

63
00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:43.790
<v Speaker 2>And in order to do that, he would, he made it a habit to go to this cave within a mountain called Jabal al-Noor, which literally means the mountain of light.

64
00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:49.150
<v Speaker 2>And the cave itself was called Hira, which means darkness.

65
00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:50.910
<v Speaker 2>And I find that interesting, right?

66
00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:55.630
<v Speaker 2>A cave named darkness within a mountain named the mountain of light.

67
00:06:57.230 --> 00:07:11.630
<v Speaker 2>Anyway, so he gets into this regular practice of contemplation and reflection, which today we call meditation or Tahannus, which is emptying the mind.

68
00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:21.950
<v Speaker 2>The word tahanas comes from hence, which in Arabic means shirk, attributing partners with God.

69
00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:31.950
<v Speaker 2>In Surah Al-Waqia, Allah says, These people, they insist on committing great shirk.

70
00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:36.670
<v Speaker 2>So, hence is shirk, attributing partners.

71
00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.910
<v Speaker 2>In Arabic morphology,

72
00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:47.710
<v Speaker 2>There's something called Baba Tafaal, where you add a taa before the word and the meaning morphs into avoidance of.

73
00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:53.070
<v Speaker 2>So hence, as it's used in the verse, hence means shirk.

74
00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:56.190
<v Speaker 2>Tahannus means the avoidance of shirk.

75
00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:00.910
<v Speaker 2>Jannb means side or nearness.

76
00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:04.430
<v Speaker 2>Tajannub means to avoid somebody's nearness.

77
00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:08.350
<v Speaker 2>So there are a lot of words like hajud, which means sleep.

78
00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:10.990
<v Speaker 2>Pahajjud means to avoid sleep.

79
00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:16.550
<v Speaker 2>Anyway, these are all these different words that follow the same pattern and morphology.

80
00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:26.190
<v Speaker 2>So one day, this 40 year old man, he's busy meditating in this cave at the top of this mountain.

81
00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:34.990
<v Speaker 2>And just like any other day, he gets into this deep contemplative state and his desire is

82
00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:38.270
<v Speaker 2>this attempt to empty out forms.

83
00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:41.870
<v Speaker 2>That's what he's trying to do.

84
00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:46.030
<v Speaker 2>He's trying to disconnect himself from this physical world.

85
00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:52.750
<v Speaker 2>He wants to free himself from all of these forms or attachments of this world.

86
00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:55.630
<v Speaker 2>Now, what's the greatest shirk of all?

87
00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:03.070
<v Speaker 2>What is the greatest shirk of all the greatest

88
00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:07.470
<v Speaker 2>It's the worship of the self.

89
00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:16.510
<v Speaker 2>Idolatry has nothing on self-olatry.

90
00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:17.790
<v Speaker 2>I just made that word up.

91
00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:23.230
<v Speaker 2>But really idolatry has nothing on self-worship or egocentricity.

92
00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:34.190
<v Speaker 2>According to our tradition, worship of the self, which pretty much everyone is engaged in today, it's the most destructive thing there is for the soul.

93
00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:40.110
<v Speaker 2>Allah talks about these people who worship their desires and their passions.

94
00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:48.590
<v Speaker 2>Do you not see the one who took his own desires, his own passions as his God?

95
00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:55.470
<v Speaker 2>Do you not see the one that when his desire commands, he just simply submits?

96
00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:56.510
<v Speaker 2>Because that's worship.

97
00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:03.870
<v Speaker 2>So Rasulullah Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam is in this cave in deep reflection.

98
00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:06.990
<v Speaker 2>working on himself.

99
00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:10.510
<v Speaker 2>He's trying to empty his heart out of all these attachments.

100
00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:16.990
<v Speaker 2>And even in Chinese, you have this thing, empty your cup so that it can be filled with something new.

101
00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:24.910
<v Speaker 2>Because the soul has to be emptied of all forms before it can gain knowledge of the one who has no form.

102
00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:29.030
<v Speaker 2>Because forms, they impinge on that understanding.

103
00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:38.270
<v Speaker 2>And as he's doing that, suddenly he sees the horizons filled with the presence of an angelic being.

104
00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:46.830
<v Speaker 2>And this being says to him, Muhammad, I am Jibreel and you have been chosen as the messenger of Allah.

105
00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:53.230
<v Speaker 2>So he raises his head to see who's speaking to him and he sees Jibreel filling up the horizons.

106
00:10:54.110 --> 00:11:00.430
<v Speaker 2>Now think about that for a second, if all of us stepped out right now,

107
00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:12.830
<v Speaker 2>and we saw an angelic presence from one horizon to the other and as far up in the sky as we could see, how would that affect us?

108
00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:17.150
<v Speaker 2>How would that affect us psychologically?

109
00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:19.310
<v Speaker 2>How would that affect us physically?

110
00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:22.990
<v Speaker 2>What would you and I experience in that moment?

111
00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:26.830
<v Speaker 2>Would we be terrified?

112
00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:29.230
<v Speaker 2>Would we be horrified?

113
00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:30.270
<v Speaker 2>Would we be shocked?

114
00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:32.830
<v Speaker 2>What would we experience?

115
00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:40.430
<v Speaker 2>See, the interesting thing about experience is that it is not a logical proposition.

116
00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:44.030
<v Speaker 2>You cannot prove or disprove experience.

117
00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:51.470
<v Speaker 2>You can discuss it, you can talk about it, you can try to interpret it, but you cannot say whether it's true or false.

118
00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:56.350
<v Speaker 2>Because experience is always real for the one experiencing it.

119
00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:06.670
<v Speaker 2>The challenge then is to determine whether or not that experience has an objective reality or it has a subjective reality.

120
00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:17.790
<v Speaker 2>In other words, is that experience really a true experience or is it something that is just happening to this individual as a type of psychosis?

121
00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:22.990
<v Speaker 2>Because we do have a lot of people in psychic wards today speaking to God directly, right?

122
00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:26.990
<v Speaker 2>They will tell you that they speak to God and they can hear God speaking to them.

123
00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:28.350
<v Speaker 2>These people are not lying.

124
00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:36.190
<v Speaker 2>Because even though they are in a delusional state, they actually believe what they're experiencing.

125
00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:39.310
<v Speaker 2>Their delusions are very real to them.

126
00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:49.390
<v Speaker 2>So the prophet naturally is filled with all those human emotions, fear, shock, horror.

127
00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:52.590
<v Speaker 2>So he turns his face away from him.

128
00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:57.470
<v Speaker 2>But no matter where he turns, he sees the angel in the exact same form.

129
00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.230
<v Speaker 2>And then Jibreel says to him, recite.

130
00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:07.310
<v Speaker 2>And he replies, What should I recite?

131
00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:08.430
<v Speaker 2>I don't know what to recite.

132
00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:16.590
<v Speaker 2>And at that point, Jibreel wraps his wings around him and he squeezes him three times.

133
00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:23.150
<v Speaker 2>And according to the Prophet, this squeeze was so hard,

134
00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:27.950
<v Speaker 2>that he felt stifled to the point where he thought he would die.

135
00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:42.590
<v Speaker 2>And scholars have commented that this squeezing was a physical manifestation of a spiritual reality which was removing any and all remnants of this world from his soul.

136
00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:52.910
<v Speaker 2>He was being emptied out in order to later be filled with three divine vessels: Islam, Iman, and Ihsan.

137
00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:59.710
<v Speaker 2>And so there had to be a complete emptying out before the filling could take place.

138
00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:02.750
<v Speaker 2>And this is what he was seeking.

139
00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:07.710
<v Speaker 2>That's what he was trying to do to begin with, with that meditation in .

140
00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:16.910
<v Speaker 2>And also this is what the companions used to say about the Prophet that he would literally empty us out of ignorance of Jahiliyya and then fill us up with Islam.

141
00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:19.630
<v Speaker 2>Right?

142
00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:22.590
<v Speaker 2>And this is why La ilaha illallah starts with a negation.

143
00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:24.830
<v Speaker 2>there is no God.

144
00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:31.470
<v Speaker 2>It starts with a negation for emptying out, breaking false pretenses, breaking false understandings.

145
00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:34.910
<v Speaker 2>Then it says illallah which is an affirmation.

146
00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:40.750
<v Speaker 2>So every Islam always began with, every religion began with an emptying out.

147
00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:48.590
<v Speaker 2>And so even for Rasool Allah Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam, Islam was actually a complete emptying before it was a filling.

148
00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:51.790
<v Speaker 2>And this in Arabic is called Takhliya.

149
00:14:53.950 --> 00:15:01.550
<v Speaker 2>The Arabs say, That the emptying out must precede the ornaments.

150
00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:09.070
<v Speaker 2>In other words, you have to be stripped off of what is ugly before you can be ornamented with what is beautiful.

151
00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:14.270
<v Speaker 2>So that squeeze was meant to empty his soul out.

152
00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:17.790
<v Speaker 2>And then he says, then he starts filling him with wahi.

153
00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:22.510
<v Speaker 2>Qalaqal insanam min alaq.

154
00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:24.590
<v Speaker 2>Yaqra warabbukal akram.

155
00:15:24.830 --> 00:15:26.350
<v Speaker 2>Alladi alama bil qalam.

156
00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:28.510
<v Speaker 2>Allamal insanam alam yalam.

157
00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:29.550
<v Speaker 2>Recite.

158
00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:33.670
<v Speaker 2>Recite in the name of your Lord who created you.

159
00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:36.910
<v Speaker 2>Created man from a clinging substance.

160
00:15:38.030 --> 00:15:39.390
<v Speaker 2>Yaqra warabbukal akram.

161
00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:41.950
<v Speaker 2>Recite for your Lord is the most generous.

162
00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:43.710
<v Speaker 2>Alladi alama bil qalam.

163
00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:45.550
<v Speaker 2>Who taught by the means of the pen.

164
00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:50.910
<v Speaker 2>Who taught man that which he did not know.

165
00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:57.470
<v Speaker 2>So now, the Prophet moves into a new realm.

166
00:15:58.790 --> 00:16:00.830
<v Speaker 2>And a troublesome realm, I think.

167
00:16:01.270 --> 00:16:15.950
<v Speaker 2>Because for every human being, for every intellectual human being at least, one of the fundamental crises of religion becomes, is my object of devotion indeed a transcendent reality?

168
00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:21.230
<v Speaker 2>Or is it some kind of a distortion of my human consciousness?

169
00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:24.510
<v Speaker 2>In other words, is this experience really true?

170
00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:28.790
<v Speaker 2>Or am I having some kind of a psychological breakdown?

171
00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:36.990
<v Speaker 2>Now, how do you differentiate between a true divine experience and a demonic experience?

172
00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:39.310
<v Speaker 2>And this is key.

173
00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:42.830
<v Speaker 2>So pay attention to this because

174
00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:48.830
<v Speaker 2>This is the key that differentiates between a demonic experience and a true spiritual experience.

175
00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:55.710
<v Speaker 2>A demonic experience is always accompanied with an expansion of the self.

176
00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:11.310
<v Speaker 2>A demonic experience always begins with, you know, it's a very exciting, very joyful experience, very pleasurable experience, but then it always inevitably

177
00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:14.270
<v Speaker 2>It results in pain and despair in the end.

178
00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:19.030
<v Speaker 2>And you can think of anything that Islam deems haram, right?

179
00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:25.150
<v Speaker 2>Drugs, alcohol, sexual deviancy, smoking, any kind of overindulgence.

180
00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:40.750
<v Speaker 2>They all begin with a rush, an excitement, ecstasy, can't stop feeling high, euphoria, but it ends, always ends.

181
00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:42.030
